    <string name="setting_hd_mode">HD Radio</string>   <!-- M11 -->
    <string name="setting_hd_mode_help">Auto plays the analog at once and blends to the HD audio when it is good, like a car radio - and back before a dropout. On AM the sound runs about 3 seconds behind live in Auto, so the blend has time to line up. Digital only is HD only (silence without HD). Analog only switches the HD decoder off (saves battery).</string>   <!-- 11d; 12a step 3; build 19: AM has analog + blend now -->
    <string name="setting_fm_stereo">Stereo</string>   <!-- 11b step 2; build 19: "FM stereo" -> "Stereo" (it serves C-QUAM AM stereo too) -->
    <string name="setting_fm_stereo_help">FM stereo and C-QUAM AM stereo. Auto blends to mono as the signal gets noisy, like a car radio (ST on the signal line = stereo). Always mono cuts the hiss of weak DX stations.</string>
    <string name="setting_am_bandwidth">AM audio bandwidth</string>   <!-- build 19 -->
    <string name="setting_am_bandwidth_help">How much of the AM audio is let through. 5 kHz suits most stations, and HD stations send no more on analog. Wider sounds fuller on a strong station without HD, but lets in more whistles from the stations next door.</string>
    <!-- 1.2: rtl_tcp -->
    <string name="settings_source">Source</string>
    <string name="setting_source">Radio source</string>
    <string name="setting_tcp_host">rtl_tcp server</string>
    <string name="setting_tcp_port">rtl_tcp port</string>
    <string name="setting_tcp_host_hint">e.g. 192.168.1.20 or raspberrypi.local</string>
    <string name="setting_source_help">rtl_tcp: the dongle sits on a PC or Raspberry Pi running rtl_tcp, and the phone gets the samples over your Wi-Fi - the antenna can be anywhere, and the phone can charge while it plays. It needs a steady 24 Mbit/s, so use 5 GHz Wi-Fi or a wired server. This is the only thing the app uses the network for. On Android 17 and later, Android asks once for \"Nearby devices\" (local network access) the first time you start the radio this way. A dongle plugged into the phone always comes first.</string>
